---
import StandardPageLayout from "../../layouts/StandardPageLayout.astro";
import PageToc from "../../components/PageToc.astro";
import "../../styles/standards-poster.css";
import "../../styles/extended.css";
import metadata from "../../data/standards/asbj/asbj30.json";
---

<StandardPageLayout
  code={metadata.code}
  standardType={metadata.framework}
  title={metadata.title}
  description={metadata.description}
  issued={metadata.issued}
  latestAmendment={metadata.latestAmendment}
  status={metadata.status}
  summary={metadata.summary}
>
  <Fragment slot="toc">
    <PageToc
      items={[
        { id: "s1", label: "Conceptual Framework Linkage" },
        { id: "s2", label: "Scope" },
        { id: "s3", label: "Fair Value Defined — an Exit Price Notion" },
        { id: "s4", label: "The Three-Level Fair Value Hierarchy" },
        { id: "s5", label: "Unifying Previously Scattered Guidance" },
        { id: "s6", label: "A NOTABLE CONTRAST — Another Case of Direct Current-Standard Adoption" },
        { id: "s7", label: "Worked Example — Classifying a Level 1 Measurement" },
        { id: "s8", label: "Worked Example — Classifying a Level 3 Measurement" },
        { id: "s9", label: "Disclosure Requirements" },
        { id: "s10", label: "Critical Judgments & Estimates" },
        { id: "s11", label: "Relationship to IAS/IFRS" },
        { id: "s12", label: "Statement Mapping" },
        { id: "s13", label: "Related Standards" },
        { id: "s14", label: "Glossary" },
      ]}
    />
  </Fragment>

  <div class="wrap">

    <div class="section-header" id="s1"><span class="num">1</span><h2>Conceptual Framework Linkage</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card ci span2">
        <div class="ctitle ci">A single, consistent definition and hierarchy across all of J-GAAP</div>
        <div class="principle">ASBJ Statement No. 30 establishes a SINGLE, UNIFIED definition and MEASUREMENT hierarchy for "FAIR VALUE" (時価) APPLICABLE across EVERY other J-GAAP standard that REQUIRES a fair value measurement — financial instruments (ASBJ Statement No. 10), business combinations (ASBJ Statement No. 21), impairment testing, and more — REPLACING what was PREVIOUSLY scattered, standard-by-standard fair value guidance WITHOUT a single, consistent framework.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s2"><span class="num">2</span><h2>Scope</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cw span2">
        <div class="ctitle cw">Applies wherever another J-GAAP standard requires fair value measurement</div>
        <div class="principle">This Statement applies WHEREVER another J-GAAP standard REQUIRES (or permits) a fair value measurement — it does NOT itself DETERMINE which items should be measured at fair value (that remains the ROLE of the individual standard concerned), but rather supplies the CONSISTENT definition and methodology for HOW that fair value measurement should be PERFORMED.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s3"><span class="num">3</span><h2>Fair Value Defined — an Exit Price Notion</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cd span2">
        <div class="ctitle cd">Directly mirroring IFRS 13's definition</div>
        <div class="text-supporting">FAIR VALUE is DEFINED as the price that WOULD be RECEIVED to SELL an asset, or PAID to TRANSFER a liability, in an ORDERLY transaction between MARKET PARTICIPANTS at the MEASUREMENT date — a genuine "EXIT PRICE" notion, DIRECTLY MIRRORING IFRS 13's own DEFINITION internationally, WORD-FOR-WORD in SUBSTANCE.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s4"><span class="num">4</span><h2>The Three-Level Fair Value Hierarchy</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cs span2">
        <div class="ctitle cs">Prioritising observable market inputs over unobservable estimates</div>
        <div class="td-table">
          <div class="th cols-06-18">
            <span>Level</span>
            <span>Description</span>
          </div>
          <div class="tr cols-06-18"><span>Level 1</span><span>QUOTED prices in ACTIVE markets for IDENTICAL assets/liabilities — the HIGHEST quality, most reliable input</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt cols-06-18"><span>Level 2</span><span>OBSERVABLE inputs OTHER than quoted prices (e.g. quoted prices for SIMILAR assets, or observable market data such as interest rates/yield curves)</span></div>
          <div class="tr cols-06-18"><span>Level 3</span><span>UNOBSERVABLE inputs, used ONLY when observable market data is NOT available — requiring the GREATEST degree of judgement</span></div>
        </div>
        <div class="note">This THREE-LEVEL hierarchy DIRECTLY MIRRORS IFRS 13's OWN structure internationally.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s5"><span class="num">5</span><h2>Unifying Previously Scattered Guidance</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cd span2">
        <div class="ctitle cd">A single reference point, replacing standard-by-standard fair value rules</div>
        <div class="text-supporting">BEFORE this Statement, fair value MEASUREMENT guidance was SCATTERED across the INDIVIDUAL J-GAAP standards that HAPPENED to require it — WITHOUT a SINGLE, consistent DEFINITION or HIERARCHY applicable UNIFORMLY across ALL of them. ASBJ Statement No. 30 CONSOLIDATED this into ONE unified reference framework — CLOSELY paralleling how IFRS 13 ITSELF consolidated PREVIOUSLY scattered fair value guidance that existed across MULTIPLE individual IFRS standards before ITS OWN introduction internationally.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s6"><span class="num">6</span><h2>A NOTABLE CONTRAST — Another Case of Direct Current-Standard Adoption</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cw span2">
        <div class="ctitle cw">Alongside Revenue Recognition, a genuine "kept pace" example</div>
        <div class="text-supporting">LIKE ASBJ Statement No. 29 (Revenue Recognition), this Statement represents a case where J-GAAP DIRECTLY ADOPTED the CURRENT international framework (IFRS 13) from the OUTSET, RATHER than remaining anchored to an OLDER, SCATTERED approach for an extended period. TOGETHER, these two Statements illustrate that J-GAAP's convergence PACE is genuinely UNEVEN across TOPIC areas — SOME standards (financial instruments, leases, insurance-adjacent areas) have LAGGED behind superseded international models for YEARS, while OTHERS (revenue recognition, fair value measurement) were built DIRECTLY around the CURRENT standard from the START.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s7"><span class="num">7</span><h2>Worked Example — Classifying a Level 1 Measurement</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cw span2">
        <div class="ctitle cw">The highest-quality fair value input</div>
        <div class="worked worked--flush">
          <div class="wk-title">Example: Listed equity securities</div>
          <div class="wk-body">A company holds shares in a company listed on the Tokyo Stock Exchange, with a readily available, actively traded quoted market price.</div>
          <div class="wk-answer">This is classified as a <strong>LEVEL 1</strong> fair value measurement — the QUOTED market price for an IDENTICAL asset in an ACTIVE market provides the HIGHEST-quality, most DIRECTLY observable fair value input available.</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s8"><span class="num">8</span><h2>Worked Example — Classifying a Level 3 Measurement</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card ci span2">
        <div class="ctitle ci">Relying on unobservable inputs</div>
        <div class="worked worked--flush">
          <div class="wk-title">Example: An unlisted equity investment</div>
          <div class="wk-body">A company holds a small equity stake in an unlisted start-up, with no observable market price and no comparable recent transactions. Fair value is estimated using a discounted cash flow model based on internally projected future cash flows.</div>
          <div class="wk-answer">This is classified as a <strong>LEVEL 3</strong> fair value measurement — since the valuation relies on genuinely UNOBSERVABLE inputs (management's own internal projections), requiring the GREATEST degree of JUDGEMENT among the three hierarchy levels.</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s9"><span class="num">9</span><h2>Disclosure Requirements</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cd span2">
        <div class="ctitle cd">What ASBJ Statement No. 30 requires</div>
        <div class="check-list">
          <span>Fair value hierarchy level for each class of asset/liability measured at fair value</span>
          <span>Valuation techniques and significant unobservable inputs used for Level 3 measurements</span>
          <span>Transfers between hierarchy levels during the period</span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s10"><span class="num">10</span><h2>Critical Judgments &amp; Estimates</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cn span2">
        <div class="ctitle cn">Where judgement matters most</div>
        <div class="two-col">
          <div>
            <div class="elabel">SIGNIFICANT JUDGEMENTS</div>
            <div class="dot-list">
              <span>Correctly classifying a fair value measurement within the appropriate hierarchy level</span>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div>
            <div class="elabel">SIGNIFICANT ESTIMATES</div>
            <div class="dot-list">
              <span>Unobservable inputs and valuation model assumptions used for Level 3 measurements</span>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s11"><span class="num">11</span><h2>Relationship to IAS/IFRS</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card ci span2">
        <div class="ctitle ci">Comparison — J-GAAP vs IFRS 13</div>
        <div class="td-table">
          <div class="th cols-12-12-12">
            <span>Feature</span>
            <span style="color:var(--color-text-warning);">J-GAAP (ASBJ Statement No. 30)</span>
            <span style="color:var(--color-text-info);">IFRS (IFRS 13)</span>
          </div>
          <div class="tr cols-12-12-12"><span>Fair value definition</span><span>Exit price notion</span><span>Exit price notion — converged</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt cols-12-12-12"><span>Hierarchy structure</span><span>Three levels (1, 2, 3)</span><span>Three levels (1, 2, 3) — converged</span></div>
        </div>
        <div class="note">This is ONE of the MOST thoroughly converged, genuinely UNIFIED standards in the ENTIRE J-GAAP suite — a DIRECT, CURRENT-standard adoption rather than a lagged or partially converged treatment.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s12"><span class="num">12</span><h2>Statement Mapping</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cs span2">
        <div class="ctitle cs">Where every fair value measurement lands</div>
        <div class="td-table">
          <div class="th cols-16-1-14">
            <span>Event</span>
            <span>Statement(s) affected</span>
            <span>Notes</span>
          </div>
          <div class="tr cols-16-1-14"><span>Any fair value measurement</span><span>Varies by underlying item</span><span>The methodology is uniform; the STATEMENT effect follows the specific item's own governing standard</span></div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s13"><span class="num">13</span><h2>Related Standards</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cd span2">
        <div class="ctitle cd">Cross-reference map</div>
        <div class="td-table">
          <div class="th cols-08-2">
            <span>Standard</span>
            <span>Connection</span>
          </div>
          <div class="tr cols-08-2"><span>ASBJ Statement No. 10</span><span>Financial Instruments — a primary consumer of this Statement's fair value framework</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt cols-08-2"><span>ASBJ Statement No. 21</span><span>Business Combinations — relies on this Statement's fair value methodology for acquisition accounting</span></div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s14"><span class="num">14</span><h2>Quick-Reference Glossary</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cn span2">
        <div class="glossary-item">
          <div class="glossary-term">Fair value (時価)</div>
          <div class="glossary-def">The price that would be received to sell an asset or paid to transfer a liability in an orderly transaction between market participants at the measurement date.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="glossary-item glossary-item--last">
          <div class="glossary-term">Fair value hierarchy</div>
          <div class="glossary-def">A three-level classification (observable to unobservable inputs) indicating the reliability of a fair value measurement.</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="legend">
      <div class="ltitle">Financial statement key (Japan GAAP terminology)</div>
      <div class="lrow">
        <span class="badge bs">BS</span> Balance Sheet (貸借対照表)
        <span class="badge bp">P&amp;L</span> Statement of Income / Profit and Loss Statement (損益計算書)
        <span class="badge bo">Net Assets</span> 純資産の部
        <span class="badge be">SCNA</span> Statement of Changes in Net Assets (株主資本等変動計算書)
        <span class="badge bc">SCF</span> Statement of Cash Flows (キャッシュ・フロー計算書)
      </div>
    </div>

  </div>
</StandardPageLayout>
